(7672855, 1253210, 60581, '<td valign="top">I realize after submitting the image :D there is no excuse for that but yes it was a mistake :)<br/> <br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by MaryO:</b></div><hr/><i> Your horizon is a bit tilted; was that intentional? I can''t tell. </i></td></tr></table></td>', 'I realize after submitting the image :D there is no excuse for that but yes it was a mistake :) Originally posted by MaryO: Your horizon is a bit tilted; was that intentional? I can''t tell. ', '2020-08-22 11:28:42.000000', NULL, 0),
(7672856, 1253210, 60581, '<td valign="top">Thank you so much Mark for the details and yes I agreed, I made some mistakes and really took down the shot. I will keep your suggestion in mind next time when I am going to take the shots! Again thank you such mark!<br/> <br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by mpeters:</b></div><hr/><i> A couple of comments -- take them from a my perspective as an aspiring landscape photographer. The scene is pleasing and looks like it has potential for sure. Suggest bringing down the green saturation on the rocks. Always check your horizon - on DPC(and elsewhere), crooked horizons grab the viewers attention and that typically isn''t the photographer''s intent. Regarding your positioning/composition - suggest a camera location to the right of pictured. You could have kept the vivid green rocks in the frame but used the line of breaking surf to create a stronger leading diagonal from foreground to background. The water movement around the rocks in the sea would have been retained and although the sun would may have moved to the right in your composition, I believe the overall composition would have been improved.<br/> <br/>One mans opinion. Happy shooting! </i></td></tr></table></td>', 'Thank you so much Mark for the details and yes I agreed, I made some mistakes and really took down the shot. I will keep your suggestion in mind next time when I am going to take the shots! Again thank you such mark! Originally posted by mpeters: A couple of comments -- take them from a my perspective as an aspiring landscape photographer. The scene is pleasing and looks like it has potential for sure. Suggest bringing down the green saturation on the rocks. Always check your horizon - on DPC(and elsewhere), crooked horizons grab the viewers attention and that typically isn''t the photographer''s intent. Regarding your positioning/composition - suggest a camera location to the right of pictured. You could have kept the vivid green rocks in the frame but used the line of breaking surf to create a stronger leading diagonal from foreground to background. The water movement around the rocks in the sea would have been retained and although the sun would may have moved to the right in your composition, I believe the overall composition would have been improved. One mans opinion. Happy shooting! ', '2020-08-22 11:29:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(7672862, 1253209, 100831, '<td valign="top">Nice rich colors and great details!  So pretty!</td>', 'Nice rich colors and great details!  So pretty!', '2020-08-22 11:53:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(7672863, 1253229, 173844, '<td valign="top">Just one comment on this fascinating inner landscape, so I''m drawn out, still dreaming, of my cave.  The bridge rises, let''s us peer at the workings behind the stolid visage.  Beyond the philosophical complexity is the artistry of the image, all the delicate intersections.  His eyeglasses alone are a marvel, functioning as windows and riverfront, or oceanfront, as the earpiece angles, then melts, into the city.  His left lens is subtly unbounded.  How beautifully his cheek merges with the water.  The vegetation above his right ear acts as a vignette, herding us within.  Etc.  And the muted palette is quite satisfying, as is the overall texturing.  A tour de force.<br/>One of my top three, with an 8.  My miserly mood was a disservice, sorry, and I''d like to asterisk this as a 10, and at least welcome it among my favorites.</td>', 'Just one comment on this fascinating inner landscape, so I''m drawn out, still dreaming, of my cave.  The bridge rises, let''s us peer at the workings behind the stolid visage.  Beyond the philosophical complexity is the artistry of the image, all the delicate intersections.  His eyeglasses alone are a marvel, functioning as windows and riverfront, or oceanfront, as the earpiece angles, then melts, into the city.  His left lens is subtly unbounded.  How beautifully his cheek merges with the water.  The vegetation above his right ear acts as a vignette, herding us within.  Etc.  And the muted palette is quite satisfying, as is the overall texturing.  A tour de force.One of my top three, with an 8.  My miserly mood was a disservice, sorry, and I''d like to asterisk this as a 10, and at least welcome it among my favorites.', '2020-08-22 11:56:19.000000', NULL, 0),
